WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.937
Delegation is more than telling someone to handle it.

2
00:00:03.137 --> 00:00:07.447
Suppose the director asks the programs lead to
coordinate the next workshop.

3
00:00:07.647 --> 00:00:12.664
A useful assignment identifies the result: one confirmed
schedule and a ready room.

4
00:00:12.864 --> 00:00:18.189
It also supplies decision authority, such as adjusting
preparation times within agreed staffing.

5
00:00:18.389 --> 00:00:21.776
The limits matter too: no new spending commitment
without approval.

6
00:00:21.976 --> 00:00:26.521
Resources include time, information, and access to the
people doing the work.

7
00:00:26.721 --> 00:00:30.156
A brief check-in provides support and a way to raise
problems.

8
00:00:30.356 --> 00:00:33.759
The manager retains responsibility for appropriate
oversight.

9
00:00:33.959 --> 00:00:40.198
Taking every small decision back undermines delegation;
disappearing entirely is not the alternative.

10
00:00:40.398 --> 00:00:47.206
Unity of command is the classical principle that one
supervisor gives an employee assignments and evaluates
the work.

11
00:00:47.406 --> 00:00:54.310
Where multiple reporting relationships exist, employees
need clear priorities and a way to resolve competing
directions.

12
00:00:54.510 --> 00:01:00.792
Naming a workshop coordinator does not silently give
that person unlimited authority over every department.

13
00:01:00.992 --> 00:01:05.078
Make the relationship explicit before the first
conflicting request arrives.

